/-!
# The rational-covering site on complete Huber pairs

This file equips the opposite category `CAff.{u}ᵒᵖ` of complete Hausdorff Huber pairs with the
Grothendieck topology generated by finite rational coverings — prior to assuming that the structure
presheaf of `Spa(A, A⁺)` is a sheaf — and defines the site-theoretic sheafiness predicate
`CAff.IsRationalSheafy`.

## Main definitions

* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization`: the intrinsic universal property characterizing a morphism
  `φ : X ⟶ Y` in `CAff` as a completed rational localization of `X` at `(T, s)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.rationalLocalization` and `TauCeti.CAff.rationalLocalizationHom`: the canonical
  completed rational localization `(A⟨T/s⟩, A_U⁺)` in `CAff` for an open span `(T)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.rationalLocalizationRingEquiv`: identifies `(X.rationalLocalization T s hT).ring`
  with the completed localization `A⟨T/s⟩`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.rationalLocalizationRestrict`: the canonical restriction morphism between
  canonical rational localizations along a containment `R(T'/s') ⊆ R(T/s)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalCovering`: a finite family `(T i, s i)` with open numerator spans whose
  rational subsets cover `Spa(X)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.rationalCoverPresieve`: the presieve on `op X` generated by the canonical rational
  localizations of a family `(T i, s i)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.rationalCoverage` and `TauCeti.CAff.ratTopology`: the rational coverage on
  `CAff.{u}ᵒᵖ` and its associated Grothendieck topology.
* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalSheafy`: `CAff.globalSections` satisfies `IsSheafFor` on all rational
  coverings of all rational localizations of `X`.

## Main results

* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.lift`, `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.hom_ext`,
  `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.epi`, `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.restrict`,
  `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.iso`, and `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.isPullback`:
  universal factorization, epimorphism property, restriction maps, canonical isomorphism, and
  intersection pullbacks for arbitrary rational localizations.
* `TauCeti.CAff.isRationalLocalization_rationalLocalizationHom`: the canonical morphism
  `X ⟶ X.rationalLocalization T s hT` satisfies `IsRationalLocalization` (Wedhorn Lemma 8.1
  upgraded to Huber pairs via `comap_mem_spa_completedPlusSubring`).
* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.isOpenEmbedding_spaComap` and
  `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalLocalization.range_spaComap`: any rational localization induces an open
  embedding on `Spa` with range `R(T/s)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.isPullback_rationalLocalizationRestrict`: the canonical localization of an
  intersection of two rational subsets is a pullback in `CAffᵒᵖ`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalCovering.pullback_of_isOpen` and
  `TauCeti.CAff.IsRationalCovering.pullback`: morphisms mapping numerator ideals to open ideals
  (in particular adic morphisms) pull rational coverings back directly, whereas
  `CAff.rationalCoverage` refines arbitrary morphisms in `CAff` using the rational basis and
  quasi-compactness of `Spa`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.isSheaf_iff`: a presheaf valued in any category on `CAffᵒᵖ` is a sheaf for
  `ratTopology` if and only if it satisfies `Presieve.IsSheafFor` objectwise on every
  `X.rationalCoverPresieve T s hcov.isOpen_span`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.sieve_ofArrows_eq_generate_rationalCoverPresieve` and
  `TauCeti.CAff.sieve_ofArrows_mem_ratTopology`: covering sieves for arbitrary families of
  rational localizations covering `Spa(X)`.
* `TauCeti.CAff.exists_mem_range_spaComap_of_mem_ratTopology` and
  `TauCeti.CAff.bot_mem_ratTopology_iff`: every covering sieve in `ratTopology (op X)` is jointly
  surjective on `Spa(X)`, and `⊥ ∈ ratTopology (op X) ↔ Subsingleton X.ring`.

## References

* P. Scholze, *Étale cohomology of diamonds*, arXiv:1709.07343v3, §2–3 and §7.
* P. Scholze and J. Weinstein, *Berkeley Lectures on `p`-adic Geometry*, Annals of Mathematics
  Studies 207, Princeton University Press (2020), Proposition 4.3.4 and §4–5.
* [T. Wedhorn, *Adic Spaces*][wedhorn_adic] (arXiv:1910.05934v1), §7.3, §8.1–8.2 (Lemma 8.1,
  Proposition 8.2, Remark 8.4).
-/

